{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZeHG2dkL2tgRZeYiAzA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 08 (edge separation) is done and the gate passes: `lake build` is clean, the only `sorry` is the one already allowed in `Sandwich.lean`, and 1,231 tests pass.\n\n`lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/EdgeSeparation.lean` is imported from the root and has no `sorry`. Its theorems use only the three standard axioms (`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`). Each definition is taken from Lengauer (1981) and cited by page, and none mentions bags or pathwidth.\n\n- **Lengauer's vertex separator game (VSG):** it equals `max(1, vs)`. So VSG = vs = pw for every graph with an edge, and VSG = 1 on edgeless graphs. The cut after move `i + 1` is exactly the development's `activeSuffix` at `i`.\n- **Theorem 4:** adding a triangle on every edge raises the value by exactly one, `vs(G_du) = vs(G) + 1` (so `pw(G_du) = pw(G) + 1`), for any graph with an edge. It is also proved in Lengauer's own form: `(G, K)` is positive iff `(G_du, K + 1)` is.\n- **New finding:** Theorem 4 as Lengauer states it fails at `K = 0` on edgeless graphs. There `(G_du, 1)` is positive but `(G, 0)` is not, because he requires `K` to be positive. This is proved in Lean as `isPositiveVSG_triangleGraph_counterexample`, and the correct statement assumes an edge or `K > 0`.\n- **How it was proved:** the upper half uses Lengauer's construction. The lower half does not use his normal-form Lemma 5; it is a direct induction along the layout that a layout of `G_du` induces on the original vertices.\n- **The cutwidth readings of \"edge separation\" are false:**\n  - `pw ≤ cw` always holds, so the failure is only from above.\n  - For stars, `n ≤ 2·cw(K_{1,n})`, `n ≤ 2·mcw(K_{1,n}) + 2`, and `pw(K_{1,n}) ≤ 1`.\n  - Hence `cw(K_{1,7}) > pw + 2` and `mcw(K_{1,9}) > pw + 2`.\n  - Neither cutwidth nor modified cutwidth is within any fixed constant of pathwidth.\n\nI left three things out on purpose:\n- Theorems 2–3, on black–white pebbling, which is not a Table 1 problem.\n- Theorem 7, the NP-hardness construction.\n- Exact cutwidth values on stars; only the lower bounds are needed for the counterexamples.\n\n`paper2/equivalences.md` has rows 11 and 12 updated and a new \"Item 08\" section. PROGRESS.md has the Iteration 8 entry and now reads 8/14 SOLVED, and item 08 is marked `[x]` in iterations.md. Six items remain, so the loop is not complete. Nothing was committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1866,"cache_read_input_tokens":174266,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1866,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":890,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b7fb8c0b-3bdd-4f9f-8a40-9b4ec06bbc01","uuid":"18dff016-7990-424b-9cec-feb2e76793c1","timestamp":"2026-09-30T12:43:29.666Z","request_id":"req_011CfZeHFa6dsPMwx1W95wEH"}
